{"hash":"ddef63d64b815de3","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":85.945,"said":"Har Har Mahadev! Come, walk with me. You have entered the court of Shri Grishneshwar, in the village of Verul in Maharashtra. Look at the temple: deep red stone, a tiered spire, walls crowded with carvings. Now look past the walls, to the flat-topped hills. About a kilometre and a half away, in those hills, are the caves of Ellora, cut into the rock over centuries by Buddhist, Hindu and Jain hands. The greatest of them, the Kailasa temple, was carved downward out of the living rock in the eighth century, under the Rashtrakuta king Krishna the First. In those days this place was called Elapura, and that is the name Adi Shankaracharya gives it in his Stotram: Ilapura. This is the twelfth Jyotirlinga, and for many pilgrims, the last. If you have walked with me from Somnath by the western sea, all the way to the snows of Kedarnath, this is where the circle closes. But the Lord did not come here because of kings or caves. He came because of one woman’s faith, and her story begins outside, at a great tank across the lane. Come, out through the south gate.\n\nDid you know? The Ellora caves, about 34 of them cut between roughly the 6th and 10th centuries, have been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1983.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1065},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1081,"end":1214}],"words":[[0.098,0.252,0,3],[0.362,0.228,4,7],[0.603,0.661,8,15],[2.139,0.433,17,21],[2.74,0.312,23,27],[3.065,0.204,28,32],[3.281,0.288,33,35],[4.435,0.192,37,40],[4.639,0.204,41,45],[4.856,0.421,46,53],[5.288,0.096,54,57],[5.397,0.3,58,63],[5.709,0.12,64,66],[5.841,0.288,67,71],[6.142,0.805,72,84],[7.151,0.108,86,88],[7.272,0.096,89,92],[7.38,0.325,93,100],[7.716,0.156,101,103],[7.885,0.373,104,109],[8.269,0.12,110,112],[8.401,0.757,113,124],[10.096,0.24,126,130],[10.349,0.108,131,133],[10.469,0.108,134,137],[10.589,0.541,138,144],[11.382,0.349,146,150],[11.743,0.216,151,154],[11.